# FORENSIC FIX (new-audit Medium finding): wp-cron-run.sh and
# wp-db-backup.sh below each now hold their own mkdir-based lock (matching
# update.sh's existing convention) before doing anything state-changing,
# so a slow run no longer overlaps with the next scheduled tick of itself.
#
# Weekly container image update check (Sunday 04:00 UTC — dry run only)
0 4 * * 0 podman auto-update --dry-run 2>&1 | logger -t podman-autoupdate
# WordPress system cron — replaces unreliable WP-Cron (which only fires on
# page loads). DISABLE_WP_CRON=true is set in WORDPRESS_CONFIG_EXTRA.
# Runs every 5 min; WordPress schedules (backups, updates, email) fire on time.
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-cron-run.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
# Daily MariaDB backup — verified logical dump (v7-13, was pipe-to-gzip).
# Now runs a dedicated script that checks mariadb-dump's own exit status,
# confirms the dump completed to its own marker, verifies the gzip
# archive, and rotates old backups ONLY after all three checks pass.
0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-db-backup.sh
# FORENSIC FIX (new-audit High finding): auto re-block the uploads-php
# PHP-execution escape hatch if it's been left open too long (see
# wp-hardening.sh's UPLOADS_PHP_MARKER / check-expiry). Silent no-op unless
# it's actually open past the limit, so this doesn't add routine noise.
*/15 * * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-hardening.sh check-expiry >/dev/null 2>&1
# Close temporary egress (wasp-egress allow, fileshare, maintenance, import
# downloads) the minute it expires. Before 10.11 nothing ran on a schedule: a
# window closed only when someone next ran wasp-egress, so one nobody looked
# at again stayed open in Squid while its own message said it had closed.
# A no-op that touches nothing when no entry has expired.
* * * * * /usr/local/bin/wasp-egress.sh expire >/dev/null 2>&1
# WordPress plugin/theme/core update check (Monday 07:00 UTC). Reports via
# syslog only when something is actually pending, so it doesn't become noise
# the operator learns to ignore. Never auto-updates -- see wp-plugins.sh for
# why that's deliberate rather than an omission.
0 7 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/wp-plugins.sh check >/dev/null 2>&1
# Daily malware and integrity scan (03:30 UTC). Runs the QUICK profile:
# structural checks, core-vs-pinned-image comparison, and database analysis.
# These are the high-signal, low-noise layers. YARA and ClamAV are excluded
# from the daily job deliberately -- YARA because a full-tree signature scan
# is heavy enough to matter on a small VM, ClamAV because of its memory
# footprint. Reports via syslog only when something is found, so a clean day
# is silent and the operator does not learn to ignore it.
30 3 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-malware-scan.sh quick >/dev/null 2>&1 || logger -t wp-malware "scan reported findings — run: wp-malware-scan.sh status"
# Weekly full scan including YARA (Sunday 03:45 UTC).
45 3 * * 0 /usr/local/bin/wp-malware-scan.sh yara >/dev/null 2>&1 || logger -t wp-malware "weekly YARA scan reported findings — run: wp-malware-scan.sh status"
# Daily vulnerability scan of installed plugins and themes (06:30 UTC).
# Wordfence Intelligence (a free token, one bulk feed queried locally) and
# WPVulnerability (no token, one request per component, so the provider sees the
# site's inventory; turn off with wp-plugins.sh vuln-source wpvulnerability off).
# Silent unless something matches a disclosed vulnerability or the scan could
# not complete.
30 6 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-vuln-cron.sh
# Weekly security report (Monday 07:15 UTC), sent whether or not anything was
# found: when each scan last ran, whether it completed, what it found, and what
# egress is open. The daily jobs are silent on a clean day, which makes a VM
# whose scans or mail stopped look exactly like a healthy one; this does not.
15 7 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/wp-vuln-cron.sh --digest >/dev/null 2>&1
# Import leftovers (daily 05:10 UTC): archives and staging copies older than
# 30 days hold a full copy of a client's site and database. Imported sets
# keep their quarantine and record. Logged to syslog as wp-import.
10 5 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-import.sh clean --stale 30 --yes >/dev/null 2>&1
# CrowdSec AppSec memory watchdog (every 5 min). Does nothing unless AppSec is
# on. Restarts CrowdSec at 448 MB, below its 512 MB cap, because AppSec memory
# can grow without bound on 1.8.x (crowdsecurity/crowdsec#4797).
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/wasp-appsec.sh watchdog >/dev/null 2>&1
# Report to a WASP Hive, if this VM has been enrolled in one (every 15 min).
# Push only: the Hive holds no way in to this VM. See docs/HIVE.md.
*/15 * * * * [ -r /etc/wp-install/hive.conf ] && /usr/local/bin/wasp-hive-report.sh send >/dev/null 2>&1
# Weekly self-test (Sunday 05:30 UTC): restore the newest backup into a
# throwaway database and verify the data is really there, and prove the
# read-only candidate account genuinely refuses writes. Weekly rather than
# daily because the restore starts a second database and copies the whole
# dump. Emails only on failure -- a guarantee that stops holding is exactly
# the thing worth interrupting someone for.
30 5 * * 0 /usr/local/bin/wasp-selftest.sh all >/tmp/wasp-selftest.out 2>&1 || { logger -t wasp-selftest "SELF-TEST FAILED — a stated guarantee is not holding"; [ -x /usr/local/bin/wp-notify.sh ] && NOTIFY_COOLDOWN_HOURS=0 /usr/local/bin/wp-notify.sh wasp-selftest "Self-test FAILED: backup restore or DB isolation" /tmp/wasp-selftest.out; }
# Weekly review prompt for vulnerability exceptions (Monday 08:00 UTC).
# An exception expires 90 days after it was accepted; 14 days' notice is
# enough to re-argue it before an update is blocked. Silent unless something
# is actually approaching, so it stays worth reading.
0 8 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/wp-hardening.sh exceptions-check >/dev/null 2>&1
# Enrich NEW login brute-force bans (every 30 min). Exits immediately unless
# CTI_ENRICH_BANS=1, which the installer only recommends with a purchased key
# -- at the free tier's 40/month this would consume the budget in a day.
# Only wpvm-login scenario bans are considered: an address that reached the
# login form is targeting this site, whereas an http-probing ban is noise.
*/30 * * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-hardening.sh cti-watch >/dev/null 2>&1
# Disk check twice daily. Silent below 80%; emails once per 48h above it.
# 80% rather than 95% deliberately: MariaDB refuses writes before the disk is
# actually full, the errors do not mention disk, and a backup that runs out of
# space leaves you with neither the space nor the backup.
0 */12 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-hardening.sh disk-check >/dev/null 2>&1
# External heartbeat every 10 minutes. Exits immediately unless a URL is
# configured. This is the only check that can detect the VM being GONE:
# everything else runs on the VM, so silence and health look identical.
# It verifies WordPress serves and MariaDB answers BEFORE pinging — a
# heartbeat proving only that cron runs would report healthy through a
# completely broken site.
*/10 * * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-notify.sh --heartbeat >/dev/null 2>&1
# TLS expiry, daily at 09:00. Silent above 14 days. Warns at 14 rather than
# 30 because Let's Encrypt renews at 30 — warning there would fire on every
# healthy certificate and be ignored within a week. At 14, automatic renewal
# has had time to run and has not.
0 9 * * * /usr/local/bin/wp-hardening.sh tls-check >/dev/null 2>&1

# Weekly file-integrity check: core and plugin files vs WordPress.org
# checksums. Detects a backdoor injected into an existing plugin, which is how
# most WordPress compromises persist. Output goes to syslog tagged
# wasp-integrity in key=value form for a SIEM to decode; a FAIL is auth.crit.
30 4 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/wp-plugins.sh verify >/dev/null 2>&1
